 were were  there that really marked the beginning  of the disclosure debate this time  around drafting and implementing the  government Securities Act of 1986  participating in various commissioned  legislative initiatives concerning the  separation of banking and brokerage and  advising the division generally advising  the division of corporate finance in  connection with its rulemaking  initiatives on prospectus delivery for  corporate securities kate has been with  the Securities and Exchange Commission  since her graduation from the University  of Kansas Law School in 1973 she has  held positions of increasing  responsibilities including counsel to  Commission of Bevis long strength an  assistant director in the device  of market regulation from my perspective  one of the best things about dealing  with Kaede is that she provides an  institutional memory in the disclosure  area in municipal finance having worked  in the area since the mid 1970s she has  had responsibility for a variety of  projects in the municipal area including  the implementation of the Securities Act  Amendments of 1975 establishing the msrb  and requiring registration of Municipal  Securities Dealers oversight of the msrb  from 1975 to 1980 and participation in  drafting both the municipal securities  full disclosure Act of 1978 and the  Commission's final report on New York  City when I first heard Kate McGuire say  some time ago  I'm from the federal government and I'm  here to help you I was naturally  skeptical all too often state and local  officials bond counsel and others  involved in municipal bonds have been  disappointed in our dealings with people  in Washington however as dean Polk noted  yesterday in his introduction of  Chairman ruder the process on the  development of the SEC guidelines turned  out to be substantially different we did  not always agree with the SEC nor  unfortunately did they always agree with  us nor did they draft a perfect proposed  rule but thanks to Kate and the staff at  the SEC the industry received a proposed  rule to comment on which formed a sound  basis for intelligent thoughtful  comments by all concerned groups and  individuals in view of the initial  drafting process I am confident that  Kate and the rest of the staff at the  Commission will carefully consider all  of the comments received will ask  questions about them and will ultimately  publish a rule that will not adversely  affect the municipal bond industry  is there for my pleasure at this time to  introduce Kate McGuire good morning this  is the first time I've ever been asked  to speak to a group to begin speaking  before 9:00 in the morning as I was  looking for a glass of orange juice this  morning I overheard a sort of an instant  review of one of yesterday's speakers  not much new no jokes this has made me a  little nervous although I have a brand  new text and I probably will have to  read a lot because of that I skip the  joke frankly I've always thought the SEC  disclaimer was an opening joke  furthermore with the FCC's budget  problems I don't know if you're aware of  it but we received slightly less than  the military bands we don't have the  resources to develop much in the way of  good material as a matter of fact the  SEC as a whole has only three jokes I  know I know you've heard them all so  I'll just hit the highlights what's the  biggest lie I'm from the government I'm  here to help how does a lawyer make it  through shark-infested waters  professional courtesy  what's the world's oldest profession  economists in the beginning there was  chaos they created it but seriously  thank you for inviting me to speak to  you today I believe this program may  have the greatest concentration of SEC  speakers on a single subject that has  ever been assembled of course each of us  will speak only for ourselves not for  the Commission the commissioners or the  staff of the Commission Chairman ruder  has already outlined for you the basics  of what we have proposed and how we got  there  Bob Colby and Eadie Pittman will be  participating in workshops today to  answer or possibly nimbly avoid  answering the tough questions you've  brought about the Commission's in this  tips I will try to provide a summary of  the feedback that we have received first  let me compliment you on the thorough  and persuasive letter that has been  submitted on your behalf as as Nabal it  was prepared by your special disclosure  committee which was chaired by Paul  Maiko and I believe that he and the  entire committee deserve your thanks and  if you want to give it to them now I'm  willing to leave the applause I have  read every comment letter but I haven't  mastered them all accordingly I hope  you'll forgive me if I don't mention  your key concern also I have a special  note for the press even at the staff  level  surprisingly the introduction to the  release and the rule received  substantial comment some commenters  suggested that because detailed default  data was included it was particularly  relevant to the Commission's proposals  and the Chairman emphasized the degree  to which he considered defaults  yesterday in his speech nevertheless I'd  like to point out to you that the  release itself suggests that the purpose  of the proposals is to improve the  accuracy and timeliness of disclosure  upon which investors rely other  commenters suggested that to greater  emphasis was given to the New York City  and whoops problems when conduit  financing have had greater default rates  commenters on the rule itself focused  particularly on the scope of the  proposed rule whether it should be  applied to all distributions of  municipal securities or only to initial  offerings or only to initial public  offerings commenters also expressed  opinions on eliminating lowering or  raising the ten million dollar threshold  in the rule most commenters argued that  the rule should not distinguish between  types of securities although there was  substantial opinion to the contrary to  the extent one type was singled out it  most often was conduit securities with a  suggestion for greater regulation of  offerings of those securities short-term  securities were also singled out most  often for exclusion from the rule  although other commenters suggested that  an exception for limited offerings to  institutional purchasers or some other  private offering exemption might better  address the types of short-term maturity  securities for which the rule was most  likely to impose unwarranted costs that  is variable rate demand notes and  municipal commercial paper while some  commenters suggested that an  institutional deal could be adequately  identified by limiting the denominations  in which the bonds were issued or the  size of positions that could be taken  for book entry securities others  suggested additional safeguards or  requirements such as the requirement  that an underwriter not know that the  purchaser  intended to resell the securities  several pointed out that parallels to  the 1933 Act might be unworkable given  state law publication requirements and  others pointed out that exceptions that  too closely paralleled the 33 act would  undercut what most argued was one of the  best features of the rule and proposal  that is its avoidance of 33 act  standards this was not a universal theme  however other commenters suggested that  virtually every 1933 act exception  should have a parallel in this  rulemaking and there was a reasonable  degree of sentiment for an intrastate  exemption the first requirement of the  proposed rule is the requirement that  underwriters obtain and review a  virtually final official statement prior  to bidding for purchasing a new issue of  securities the key policy concern with  this requirement was the fact that it  did not allow for a commitment to be  made subject to receipt of acceptable  information that is wire deals there was  also some difficulty in interpreting  whether this was a requirement for a  preliminary official statement in which  case some commenters argued much more as  customarily left out and currently the  preliminary official statement is  discretionary or whether it required  that a new document be produced in which  case many argued it was burdensome my  answer to this confusion is that for  those deals of which we have been told  there are many where there are no  differences between the POS and the  final OS the rural reverse refers to the  preliminary for the many deals where the  POS is not complete the rule is not  referring to the POS but to a final OS  that admits only information specified  in the proposed rule that's our proposal  for those who interpreted the rule in  this way there were particular  suggestions that credit enhancements and  information about a credit enhancer be  included in the category of information  that can be omitted as well as ratings  and information response to rating  agency comments information included in  response to underwriter comments  information concerning events sub  but to the time when the virtually final  OS is submitted to the underwriter and  other changes from the OS that are not  Material or that our material and would  improve disclosure lots of suggestions  given the breadth of these suggestions  some suggested that the standard be  reformulated to require something like  an OS that is materially complete at the  time of the bid or agreement to purchase  commenters also suggested various  phrases to make clear that the issuer's  opinion as to whether the document met  the standards of the rule at this stage  that is at the nearly final OS stage was  more relevant than than the opinion of  the underwriter in that connection  commenters suggested that the rule  recognized that for conduit securities  the relevant issuer may be the corporate  obligor not the governmental issuer the  second requirement of the proposed rule  is the requirement to deliver a  preliminary official statement to any  person on request there were comments  suggesting that any such requirement be  limited to potential investors and  suggestions that the term be defined to  include customers of the dealer and  other persons solicited by the dealer  there also were suggestions that more of  a definition of POS was needed than the  reference in the rule to a document  produced by the issuer for dissemination  to potential bidders and purchasers  there was substantial concern as to how  the rule would apply particularly in  competitive offerings where issuers do  not always supply sufficient copies of  the POS to potential underwriters for  distribution but instead take  responsibility for distribution  themselves they suggested that the rule  would be it would inappropriately shift  this burden from issuers to underwriters  particularly in competitive deals it was  not clear when potential underwriters  would assume the duty to provide the  documents my best answer under the terms  of their proposed rule as drafted and I  acknowledge that it is unclear is that  the obligation would begin when the  prospective underwriters received the  POS  there were also concerns that  underwriters would have a duty to  supplement the POS if it was not  accurate some suggested that concern  about the quality of disclosure in a POS  should be a reason not to forward a copy  on request others suggested safe harbors  under the anti-fraud provisions for  compliance with this requirement of the  rule or that the requirement expire upon  deposit of a POS with a central  repository no one suggested that the  rule be revised to require the  underwriter to forward any request for a  POS to the issuer yet overall the  underlying concern appeared to be that  for some offerings particularly I think  competitive offerings of big issuers who  do a lot of offerings we had directed  the requirement to the wrong participant  in the distribution process the third  requirement of the proposed rule was  that the underwriter contract with the  issuer or its agent to receive within 2  business days of the bid or agreement to  purchase a sufficient quantity of final  official statements to meet the  requirements of this rule and the rules  of the msrb some suggested that rather  than count forward from the bid or  purchase agreement the rule should  require the contract to specify that  final os's will be provided a number of  days prior to settlement with the issuer  so that it can accompany the customers  money confirmation concern was expressed  particularly by lawyers that events  could occur between the completion of  the final OS and settlement that would  require supplementation amendment or  recirculation everyone said that two  business days wasn't long enough five  seemed to be the most common suggestion  furthermore there were calls for  recognition that a POS could be  supplemented and serve as a final OS and  that a final OS need not be a single  booklet but could instead be a  collection of documents and references  to publicly available documents the  Commission has avoided specifying the  content of an official statement  but commenters expressed concern that  the rules concerning delivery might  adversely affect the evolution of the  form of an official statement the PSA  commented that the GFO a through its  guidelines was the appropriate  organization to design less burdensome  official statements that is consistent  with the recognition given to the GFO a  in this area by the Commission and the  msrb the 4th provision of the proposed  rule would require underwriters to make  available a final official statement to  any person on request commenters  suggested that such a requirement apply  only to potential investors expire after  a reasonable time and include a safe  harbor for providing dated material  pursuant to the rule most commenters  suggested that this function would be  done best by a central repository there  were also comments on the definitions  but except to the extent that I've  already touched on them they were too  detailed for me to keep you here finally  there was some adverse comment on the  notion of having any sort of  prescriptive rule some suggested that  the Commission developed voluntary safe  harbor rules that traded away liability  under the anti-fraud provisions for  providing particular disclosure at  particular times others suggested that  pressure from underwriters newly alerted  to their responsibilities by the  Commission's interpretation coupled with  the more active consideration of the  central repository supplemented by  market forces and vigorous enforcement  of msrb rule g32  would address the concerns underlined  the proposed rule while in theory that  may be true I think that it is an  overreaction to a perceived need for  more flexibility in the rule it is  probably more economical for the  Commission to create that flexibility  than to launch an enforcement program  which would motivate underwriters to  comply with the interpretation and  sorry rule g32 to the interpretation  which stated that an underwriter must  have a reasonable basis for recommending  securities it under writes and provides  comfort that for a normal competitive  bid offering such a basis could be  formed based on a confessional review of  the issuers disclosure document there  were a variety of comments at least one  commenter argued there was no basis for  suggesting that underwriters had such  duties under the anti-fraud provisions  while another suggested that there was  no basis for making any distinction  between negotiated and competitive bid  offerings some found our tradition of  dealing with the most sensitive issues  and footnotes to be distracting for  example they thought it would be better  to say in text that the interpretation  did not establish an objective standard  of recklessness and that for each  particular offering there would be  relevant factors that were not set forth  in the interpretation clarification was  requested on the treatment of credit  enhancements credit enhancers and  investors argued that a credit  enhancement should not affect the degree  of disclosure concerning the underlying  credit others suggested that in some  cases it would be possible to have no  disclosure about the underlying credit  at all many called for the Commission to  recognize and discuss the duties of  other participants in the process  particularly issuers but with respect to  financial advisers where the Commission  had done that there was concern that the  Commission give greater consideration to  the variety of services performed by  financial advisers and their role as  agents of the issuer furthermore there  were requests for clarification or  possibly rollbacks  of the Commission's statements that in  connection with competitive bid  offerings underwriters should assess the  completeness of the disclosure for  example are there audited financial  statements concern was also expressed  that because of its frequent reference  to the official statement the  interpretation implied that there was no  other way for underwriters to meet their  reasonable basis obligation  finally there were suggestions that  appropriate record-keeping requirements  be developed to allow examiner's to  ascertain the underwriters are in fact  carrying out the review necessary to  form a reasonable basis the third major  component of the Commission's initiative  was solicitation of comments on the MS  Arby's proposal for a central repository  my favorite comment on this was from  Dean Pope your past president he said  the much-discussed idea of a central  repository is much like the idea of a  balanced federal budget  everyone recognizes the need for it  everyone is theoretically in favor of it  but there is widespread disagreement as  to how to achieve it and who should get  stuck with the cost I would also add  that everyone is willing to 