re also comments on the definitions  but except to the extent that I've  already touched on them they were too  detailed for me to keep you here finally  there was some adverse comment on the  notion of having any sort of  prescriptive rule some suggested that  the Commission developed voluntary safe  harbor rules that traded away liability  under the anti-fraud provisions for  providing particular disclosure at  particular times others suggested that  pressure from underwriters newly alerted  to their responsibilities by the  Commission's interpretation coupled with  the more active consideration of the  central repository supplemented by  market forces and vigorous enforcement  of msrb rule g32  would address the concerns underlined  the proposed rule while in theory that  may be true I think that it is an  overreaction to a perceived need for  more flexibility in the rule it is  probably more economical for the  Commission to create that flexibility  than to launch an enforcement program  which would motivate underwriters to  comply with the interpretation and  sorry rule g32 to the interpretation  which stated that an underwriter must  have a reasonable basis for recommending  securities it under writes and provides  comfort that for a normal competitive  bid offering such a basis could be  formed based on a confessional review of  the issuers disclosure document there  were a variety of comments at least one  commenter argued there was no basis for  suggesting that underwriters had such  duties under the anti-fraud provisions  while another suggested that there was  no basis for making any distinction  between negotiated and competitive bid  offerings some found our tradition of  dealing with the most sensitive issues  and footnotes to be distracting for  example they thought it would be better  to say in text that the interpretation  did not establish an objective standard  of recklessness and that for each  particular offering there would be  relevant factors that were not set forth  in the interpretation clarification was  requested on the treatment of credit  enhancements credit enhancers and  investors argued that a credit  enhancement should not affect the degree  of disclosure concerning the underlying  credit others suggested that in some  cases it would be possible to have no  disclosure about the underlying credit  at all many called for the Commission to  recognize and discuss the duties of  other participants in the process  particularly issuers but with respect to  financial advisers where the Commission  had done that there was concern that the  Commission give greater consideration to  the variety of services performed by  financial advisers and their role as  agents of the issuer furthermore there  were requests for clarification or  possibly rollbacks  of the Commission's statements that in  connection with competitive bid  offerings underwriters should assess the  completeness of the disclosure for  example are there audited financial  statements concern was also expressed  that because of its frequent reference  to the official statement the  interpretation implied that there was no  other way for underwriters to meet their  reasonable basis obligation  finally there were suggestions that  appropriate record-keeping requirements  be developed to allow examiner's to  ascertain the underwriters are in fact  carrying out the review necessary to  form a reasonable basis the third major  component of the Commission's initiative  was solicitation of comments on the MS  Arby's proposal for a central repository  my favorite comment on this was from  Dean Pope your past president he said  the much-discussed idea of a central  repository is much like the idea of a  balanced federal budget  everyone recognizes the need for it  everyone is theoretically in favor of it  but there is widespread disagreement as  to how to achieve it and who should get  stuck with the cost I would also add  that everyone is willing to go to a  meeting on it beyond that there appears  to be a variety of ideas committee do  not want the SEC or the msrb to have too  great an involvement in such an effort  other strongly urged need for one or  both to mandate participation establish  standards or oversee or even carry out  operations somebody really wanted the  SEC to do it there are suggestions for  full text and summary only respond  repositories some underwriters believe  issuers should submit the official  statement everyone else believes  underwriters should submit the official  state most agree that what would be most  useful is to have issuers submit  periodic reports but few recommended  that that be required  there are also strong calls for  competition and some who see a central  repository as a natural monopoly I find  myself turning to the popular culture  for answers I think the Commission has  three choices Nancy Reagan's motto just  say no the Nike commercial just do it  and my personal favorite don't worry be  happy  thank you are there any questions  okay Thank You Kate for the thorough  analysis of the of the comments that  that were received and for some  indication of where it's going I think  those of us here would would like very  much not to worry and to be happy the  next part of the program are the  workshops which begin at 9:45 in the in  the breakout rooms thank you very much  and thank you for your attendance at the  workshop
 

